---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { getState, href } from '../../lib/data';
import { breadcrumbNode, pageGraph, webPageNode } from '../../lib/schema';

const title = 'North Carolina HOA rules for an RV or boat | RV Storage Prices';
const description =
  'North Carolina Planned Community Act, Chapter 47F, has no section that names an RV or a boat. The declaration and local ordinances still control parking. Not legal advice.';
const path = '/rules/north-carolina/';
const checked = '2026-11-19';
const carolina = getState('north-carolina');
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { label: 'HOA rules', path: '/rules/' },
  { label: 'North Carolina', path },
];
const schema = pageGraph([
  webPageNode({ title, description, path, dateModified: checked }),
  breadcrumbNode(crumbs),
]);
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path={path} schema={schema}>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>North Carolina HOA rules for an RV or boat</h1>
    <p>
      This page quotes the North Carolina General Statutes. It is not legal advice, not a ruling on
      your declaration, and not a substitute for the city or county code.
    </p>
    <p>
      Checked <time datetime={checked}>November 19, 2026</time> on the
      <a href="https://www.ncleg.gov/EnactedLegislation/Statutes/HTML/ByChapter/Chapter_47F.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">General Assembly’s copy of Chapter 47F</a>,
      the North Carolina Planned Community Act. That chapter file does not contain the words
      “recreational vehicle,” “motor home,” or “boat,” and it has no section that sets an RV or boat
      parking rule. A private community’s rules PDF is not part of the chapter.
    </p>
    <h2>The declaration and the rules power</h2>
    <p>G.S. 47F-2-103(a) says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        To the extent not inconsistent with the provisions of this Chapter, the declaration, bylaws,
        and articles of incorporation form the basis for the legal authority for the planned community
        to act as provided in the declaration, bylaws, and articles of incorporation, and the
        declaration, bylaws, and articles of incorporation are enforceable by their terms.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      G.S. 47F-3-102 opens: “Unless the articles of incorporation or the declaration expressly provides
      to the contrary, the association may do all of the following.” Item (1) is “Adopt and amend bylaws
      and rules and regulations.” Item (6) is “Regulate the use, maintenance, repair, replacement, and
      modification of common elements.” Neither item names an RV or a boat. A parking rule, if one
      exists, is in the declaration or in rules the declaration allows. This page does not read those
      documents.
    </p>
    <h2>What the chapter does not decide</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        G.S. 47F-1-102(a) says the chapter applies to planned communities created in the state on or
        after January 1, 1999, with exceptions in that section, including a community of no more than 20
        lots unless the declaration opts in. Some listed sections, including 47F-3-102(1), also apply to
        older communities unless the articles or the declaration say otherwise. This page does not
        decide which sentence applies to a particular community.
      </li>
      <li>
        G.S. 47F-1-106 says that, aside from a rule against treating a planned community differently from
        a substantially similar development, “no provision of this Chapter invalidates or modifies any
        provision of any zoning, subdivision, or building code or any other real estate use law,
        ordinance, or regulation.” A local parking ordinance still has to be read.
      </li>
      <li>
        The chapter does not grant a right to keep an RV or a boat on a driveway, and it does not void
        a covenant that forbids one.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Off-site storage is a different question</h2>
    <p>
      A facility listing in this directory is a storage lot, not a statement that an association allows
      the vehicle at the house. {carolina.cities.length} North Carolina city pages list those lots.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {
        carolina.cities.map((city) => (
          <li>
            <a href={href(`/cities/${city.id}/`)}>
              {city.city}, {city.state_code}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
